Setting: a k-linear symmetric monoidal category of modules (with unit 1) and the divided-power functors Γ^n. This fits the cote's theme, the PD de Rham complex. Grothendieck asks which linear functors F carry polynomial maps to polynomial maps, and so preserve divided-power structure.

(1) Top block. A homogeneous polynomial map X → Y of degree n (the arrow is labelled 'deg n', not 'dégén') is the same as a linear map Γ^n X → Y. He asks whether F induces a degree-n polynomial map F(X) → F(Y). This works if F comes with natural maps σ: Γ^n F(X) → F(Γ^n X): compose Γ^n F(X) → F(Γ^n X) → F(Y). In other words, F should be a lax morphism for the Γ-monad. The diagram F(Γ^n X) → F(Y), Γ^n F(X) ↑, ↗ is exactly this.

(2) Representable case F_A(X) = Hom(A, X). He wants β: Γ^n Hom(A,X) → Hom(A, Γ^n X). On the generators f^{(n)} the natural candidate is f^{(n)} ↦ Γ^n(f)∘u_n. Here u_n: A → Γ^n(A) is the image of id_A^{(n)}; by Yoneda, β is determined by u_n. The crossed-out line 'β(u^{(n)}) → Γ^n(u)' is this formula. The previous page derives the axioms on the family u_n:
- u_0 = 1 and u_1 = id;
- u_p·u_q = C(p+q, q)·u_{p+q} in the convolution algebra Hom(A, Γ*A);
- σ_{pq}∘u_{pq} = Γ^p(u_q)∘u_p.
The family (u_n) is thus a 'divided-power coalgebra' structure, for which u_n plays the role of the n-th divided power of u_1.

(3) Monoidal part. A natural bilinear pairing F_A(X) × F_B(Y) → F_C(X⊗Y) corresponds, by two-variable Yoneda, to a morphism C → A⊗B. Taking X = Y = A = B = C and (id_A, id_A) gives Δ: A → A⊗A. Under this dictionary:
- associativity of the product on F_A(X) ↔ coassociativity of Δ;
- commutativity ↔ cocommutativity;
- unit k → F_A(1) = Hom(A, 1) ↔ a counit A → 1.
So a lax symmetric monoidal structure on Hom(A, -) is the same as a cocommutative counital coalgebra structure on A.

(4) Next page. He combines the two structures: Γ*F_A(X) → F_A(Γ*X) is a map of ⊗-rings, because F_A is lax monoidal. Upshot: Hom(A, -) sends PD-algebras to PD-algebras when A is such a PD-coalgebra. The same mechanism underlies the classical fact that Hom(C, B), for C a cocommutative coalgebra and B a PD-algebra, is a PD-algebra under convolution.
